    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cancers arise from aberrant signaling pathways responsible for otherwise normal cell growth and proliferation. RAS is one of the most commonly mutated gene families in human cancers, present in nearly a quarter of all cases, and its signaling is key to cell development. Because the RAS protein lacks well-defined hydrophobic pockets for small molecule binding, it is a challenging drug target; therefore, modulating its interactors’ activities is of immense therapeutic interest. Previous research uncovered a small noncoding RNA pair, SNORD50A/B, whose deletion is associated with higher rates of tumorigenesis. Further, preliminary data showed that three vesicle transport-associated proteins (SNAREs) interacted more with KRAS in the absence of SNORD50A/B. To characterize the binding relationships among KRAS, the snoRNA pair, and SNARE proteins, far-Western blotting and proximity-ligation assay (PLA) of human lung cancers cells were conducted in this work. In order to observe the effect of the interactors (SNAREs and SNORD50A/B) on KRAS downstream pathways (e.g. ERK and AKT), phosphorylation levels were examined via Western blotting. Results showed that SNARE proteins promote KRAS activity and compete with antagonistic regulator SNORD50A/B. This research demonstrates that SNARE proteins interact with the snoRNA pair SNORD50A/B to modulate KRAS activity. Better understanding of the role of these SNARE proteins in the oncogenic KRAS signaling pathway could be influential in researching more treatment targets, and this work stands to contribute to future studies in KRAS interactor-oriented therapies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
